{-
   `X86.crc32` - the CRC-32C step, which is the one instruction `lib/Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana`
   declares that computes a named function rather than an arrangement of bits.

   One width and two conversions, and the conversions are the half worth asserting: the intrinsic is
   declared `crc32(remainder: U64, chunk: U64) -> U64` rather than with the thirty-two-bit
   accumulator a caller has, because the instruction reads its remainder from the low half of the
   destination and writes a zero-extended answer - so the `widen` and the `truncate` a caller writes
   around it are what the instruction already does, and the goldens are where that shows.

   **`@platform(x64)` on the declaration and therefore on every use.** SSE4.2 is inside x86-64-v2 and
   v2 is this backend's floor, so a native x86-64 target always has the instruction and there is no
   feature level to name; a target that is not x86-64 has no declaration at all, which is why there
   is no `.js.expect` beside this file and never will be.

   The answers are `zlib`'s Castagnoli polynomial computed by a reference implementation, not by this
   one - see the comment on each.
-}

import Native

-- One 64-bit chunk folded into the initial remainder, where the remainder is widened on the way in
-- and truncated on the way out. 0x9a4f27dc, over the eight bytes of 0x0123456789abcdef.
fn wide() -> U32 = truncate(X86.crc32(widen(4294967295 :: U32) :: U64, 81985529216486895)) :: U32

-- The same fold reached through values rather than literals, so that the goldens hold one that
-- constant folding cannot answer. Nothing folds a CRC today; the row exists so that a fold added
-- later has a fixture that says what it must answer.
fn folded(seed: U32, chunk: U64) -> U32 = truncate(X86.crc32(widen(seed) :: U64, chunk)) :: U32

-- And a second chunk folded into the first one's remainder, which is what a running checksum is.
-- 0x94b28b26, over the eight bytes of 0x0011223344556677.
fn chained() -> U32 = folded(wide(), 4822678189205111)

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = wide()
    let b = chained()

    -- 2588878812 + 2494728998 = 5083607810, which is 810 modulo a thousand. Taken modulo so that the
    -- answer fits the exit status a `.run.expect` compares.
    let total = (widen(a) :: I64) + (widen(b) :: I64)

    if folded(4294967295, 81985529216486895) != a then return -1
    return truncate(total `rem` 1000) :: Int
